---
/**
 * Long-form English homepage copy.
 *
 * Prose lives here as markup rather than in the i18n JSON: translation files
 * hold interface strings, and multi-paragraph article copy is far easier to
 * edit and review as HTML. Rendered on the English homepage only — see the
 * locale check in src/pages/[locale]/index.astro.
 */
import Charts from '@/components/charts/Charts.astro';
import TrustBento from '@/components/home/TrustBento.astro';
import HowItWorks from '@theme/components/HowItWorks.astro';
import { localizedUrl, type Locale } from '@theme/i18n/utils';

interface Props {
  locale: Locale;
}

const { locale } = Astro.props;
const url = (path: string) => localizedUrl(locale, path);

const link = 'font-medium text-[var(--accent)] hover:underline';

const dosageChart = {
  headers: ['Body weight', 'Loading dose (days 1–7)', 'Maintenance dose', 'Daily water target'],
  rows: [
    ['50 kg (110 lb)', '15 g/day — 4 × 3.8 g', '3 g/day', '2.8 L (2,800 ml)'],
    ['60 kg (132 lb)', '18 g/day — 4 × 4.5 g', '3 g/day', '2.8 L (2,800 ml)'],
    ['70 kg (154 lb)', '21 g/day — 4 × 5.3 g', '3 g/day', '2.8 L (2,800 ml)'],
    ['80 kg (176 lb)', '24 g/day — 4 × 6 g', '3 g/day', '2.8 L (2,800 ml)'],
    ['90 kg (198 lb)', '25 g/day — 4 × 6.3 g', '3 g/day', '2.8 L (2,800 ml)'],
    ['100 kg (220 lb)', '25 g/day — 4 × 6.3 g', '3 g/day', '2.8 L (2,800 ml)'],
    ['110 kg (243 lb)', '25 g/day — 4 × 6.3 g', '3.3 g/day', '2.8 L (2,800 ml)'],
    ['120 kg (265 lb)', '25 g/day — 4 × 6.3 g', '3.6 g/day', '2.9 L (2,900 ml)'],
  ],
};

const calculationSteps = [
  {
    title: 'Weigh yourself and pick a unit',
    description:
      'Enter body weight in kilograms or pounds. Weight drives every figure the calculator returns, so use a morning weight taken before food.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Multiply by 0.03 for maintenance',
    description:
      'A 70 kg (154 lb) lifter calculates 70 × 0.03 = 2.1 g. The ISSN floor of 3 g applies below that figure, and the calculator applies it automatically.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Multiply by 0.3 for loading',
    description:
      'The same 70 kg lifter calculates 70 × 0.3 = 21 g/day for 5–7 days, split into 4 servings of 5.3 g. Loading caps at 25 g/day.',
  },
];

const hclComparison = {
  headers: ['Factor', 'Creatine monohydrate', 'Creatine HCl'],
  rows: [
    ['Maintenance dose', '3–5 g/day', '1–2 g/day'],
    ['Loading phase', '0.3 g/kg/day for 5–7 days', 'Not required'],
    ['Solubility in water', 'Moderate — may settle', 'High — dissolves fully'],
    ['Published human studies', '500+', 'Fewer than 20'],
    ['Cost per effective dose', 'Lowest', '3–5× higher'],
    ['Stomach comfort at full dose', 'Bloating reported by some users', 'Smaller volume, fewer reports'],
  ],
};
---

<section class="mb-12 max-w-3xl space-y-4 text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
  <p>
    A creatine dosage calculator converts body weight, training goal, and activity level into an
    exact daily creatine dose in grams. <strong
      >The calculator above applies the 0.03 g per kilogram maintenance formula published in the
      International Society of Sports Nutrition (ISSN) position stand</strong
    >, then scales that figure for muscle growth, athletic performance, endurance, or general
    health.
  </p>
  <p>
    Calculating a dose instead of guessing solves three problems: underdosing at low body weights,
    wasted powder above the muscle creatine saturation point, and loading protocols that hand a
    55 kg (121 lb) beginner the same 20 g as a 110 kg (243 lb) powerlifter.
  </p>
  <p>
    The creatine intake calculator runs on four inputs — body weight in kilograms or pounds, training
    goal, weekly activity level, and creatine type — and returns a maintenance phase protocol, an
    optional 7-day loading phase protocol split into four servings, and a daily water target in
    millilitres and litres.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mb-12 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="mb-4 text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--text-primary)]">
    What Makes the Best Creatine Dosage Calculator
  </h2>
  <p class="mb-4 text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
    <strong>The best creatine dosage calculator uses body weight as its primary variable</strong> and
    returns the loading phase and maintenance phase as separate figures. Five features separate an accurate
    creatine dosing tool from a generic one:
  </p>
  <ol class="mb-4 space-y-3 text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
    <li class="flex gap-3">
      <span class="font-bold text-[var(--accent)]" aria-hidden="true">1.</span>
      <span
        ><strong>Weight-based dosing.</strong> Muscle creatine storage capacity tracks lean body mass,
        so a fixed 5 g output ignores the 60 kg (132 lb) spread between the lightest and heaviest lifters.</span
      >
    </li>
    <li class="flex gap-3">
      <span class="font-bold text-[var(--accent)]" aria-hidden="true">2.</span>
      <span
        ><strong>Separate loading and maintenance outputs.</strong> The two phases differ by a factor
        of 10 — 0.3 g/kg against 0.03 g/kg.</span
      >
    </li>
    <li class="flex gap-3">
      <span class="font-bold text-[var(--accent)]" aria-hidden="true">3.</span>
      <span
        ><strong>Creatine type adjustment.</strong> Creatine hydrochloride (HCl) needs roughly half the
        monohydrate dose, and a calculator that ignores the form overdoses HCl users.</span
      >
    </li>
    <li class="flex gap-3">
      <span class="font-bold text-[var(--accent)]" aria-hidden="true">4.</span>
      <span
        ><strong>Goal and activity scaling.</strong> Training volume raises creatine turnover, which
        moves the maintenance dose within the 3–5 g band.</span
      >
    </li>
    <li class="flex gap-3">
      <span class="font-bold text-[var(--accent)]" aria-hidden="true">5.</span>
      <span
        ><strong>A hydration output.</strong> Creatine pulls water into muscle cells, so the dose and
        the daily fluid target belong in the same result.</span
      >
    </li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section class="mb-12 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="mb-4 text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--text-primary)]">
    Creatine Dose by Phase: Loading and Maintenance
  </h2>
  <p class="mb-4 text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
    <strong
      >The optimal creatine dose is 3–5 g per day for maintenance and 0.3 g per kilogram of body
      weight per day for loading.</strong
    > Creatine supplementation runs in two phases, and the calculator outputs both.
  </p>

  <h3 class="mt-6 mb-2 text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)]">Loading protocol phase</h3>
  <p class="mb-4 text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
    The loading protocol phase runs 0.3 g/kg/day for 5–7 days, split into 4 servings taken with
    meals. An 80 kg (176 lb) lifter takes 24 g/day as 4 × 6 g. Loading raises muscle creatine stores
    20–40% above baseline within one week. Splitting the dose across four servings of 5–6 g each
    reduces the stomach discomfort and bloating that a single 20 g serving produces.
  </p>

  <h3 class="mt-6 mb-2 text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)]">Maintenance phase protocol</h3>
  <p class="mb-4 text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
    The maintenance phase protocol runs 3–5 g/day for as long as supplementation continues, taken as
    one serving. Maintenance holds the saturation that loading builds. Skipping the loading phase
    entirely reaches identical saturation in 3–4 weeks on 3–5 g/day, which suits anyone who wants to
    avoid the temporary water weight of a loading week. Read the full
    <a href={url('/blog/creatine-loading-phase-guide')} class={link}>creatine loading phase guide</a> for
    the week-by-week protocol.
  </p>
</section>

<TrustBento locale={locale} />

<Charts />

<section class="mb-12">
  <h3 class="mb-3 text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)]">Full creatine dosage chart</h3>
  <p class="mb-4 max-w-3xl text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
    Loading figures apply 0.3 g/kg/day and cap at 25 g/day, the ceiling above which absorption
    plateaus and excess creatine is excreted. Maintenance figures apply 0.03 g/kg/day against a 3 g
    floor. Lifters carrying high lean body mass often run the upper end of the 3–5 g range.
  </p>

  <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-[var(--border-subtle)]">
    <table class="w-full border-collapse text-sm">
      <caption class="sr-only">
        Creatine loading dose, maintenance dose and daily water target for body weights from 50 kg
        to 120 kg.
      </caption>
      <thead>
        <tr class="bg-[var(--surface-sunken)] text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
          {
            dosageChart.headers.map((header) => (
              <th scope="col" class="border-b border-[var(--border-subtle)] p-3 text-start font-semibold">
                {header}
              </th>
            ))
          }
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="tabular-nums">
        {
          dosageChart.rows.map((row) => (
            <tr class="even:bg-[var(--surface-sunken)]">
              <th scope="row" class="border-b border-[var(--border-subtle)] p-3 text-start font-medium text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
                {row[0]}
              </th>
              {row.slice(1).map((cell) => (
                <td class="border-b border-[var(--border-subtle)] p-3 text-[var(--text-secondary)]">{cell}</td>
              ))}
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-sm text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
    For a dose calculated to your exact weight rather than the nearest row, use the
    <a href={url('/creatine-dosage-by-weight')} class={link}>creatine dosage by weight calculator</a>.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mb-12 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="mb-4 text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--text-primary)]">Creatine Monohydrate Dosage: 3–5 g Per Day</h2>
  <p class="mb-4 text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
    <strong>Creatine monohydrate is dosed at 3–5 g per day for maintenance</strong> and 0.3 g/kg/day
    for a 5–7 day loading phase. Monohydrate carries more than 500 published human studies, which makes
    it the reference form against which every other type is measured.
  </p>
  <p class="mb-4 text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
    Micronized creatine and Creapure carry the same dosing. Micronization grinds the same molecule
    into smaller particles for easier mixing, and Creapure names a German-manufactured monohydrate
    held to a stated purity standard. Neither changes the gram figure the calculator returns.
    Kre-Alkalyn and creatine ethyl ester market themselves on absorption claims that published
    research has not supported against monohydrate.
  </p>
  <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
    Take monohydrate with 300–500 ml (10–17 oz) of water or juice, and stir until the powder no
    longer settles at the bottom of the glass.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mb-12 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="mb-4 text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--text-primary)]">Creatine HCl vs Monohydrate Dosage</h2>
  <p class="mb-4 text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
    <strong>Creatine HCl is dosed at 1–2 g per day against 3–5 g of monohydrate</strong> — roughly half
    the gram figure. The hydrochloride salt dissolves more completely in water, so a smaller serving
    delivers a comparable amount of absorbed creatine.
  </p>
  <div class="mb-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-[var(--border-subtle)]">
    <table class="w-full border-collapse text-sm">
      <caption class="sr-only">
        Creatine HCl compared against creatine monohydrate across dosing, solubility, research volume
        and cost.
      </caption>
      <thead>
        <tr class="bg-brand-600 text-white">
          {hclComparison.headers.map((header) => (
            <th scope="col" class="p-3 text-start font-semibold">{header}</th>
          ))}
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          hclComparison.rows.map((row, index) => (
            <tr class={index % 2 === 0 ? 'bg-[var(--surface-card)]' : 'bg-[var(--surface-sunken)]'}>
              <th scope="row" class="border-b border-[var(--border-subtle)] p-3 text-start font-medium text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
                {row[0]}
              </th>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--border-subtle)] p-3 text-[var(--text-secondary)]">{row[1]}</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--border-subtle)] p-3 text-[var(--text-secondary)]">{row[2]}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
    Monohydrate remains the better value for most lifters. HCl earns its price for anyone who
    reports bloating or stomach discomfort on a full monohydrate dose. Set the creatine type to HCl
    in the calculator above, or open the dedicated
    <a href={url('/creatine-hcl-calculator')} class={link}>creatine HCl dosage calculator</a>.
  </p>
</section>

<HowItWorks title="How to Calculate Creatine Dosage" steps={calculationSteps}>
  <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
    To calculate creatine dosage by hand, multiply body weight in kilograms by 0.03 for the daily
    maintenance dose and by 0.3 for the daily loading dose. Working in pounds, divide by 2.2 first: a
    185 lb lifter weighs 84 kg, which gives 2.5 g maintenance (raised to the 3 g floor) and 25 g
    loading. The calculator applies the floor, the 25 g loading cap, and the HCl adjustment in one
    step.
  </p>
</HowItWorks>

<section class="mb-12 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="mb-4 text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--text-primary)]">Creatine Dosage for Men, Women, Teenagers and Vegetarians</h2>
  <p class="mb-6 text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
    <strong>Body weight sets the creatine dose, not sex or age.</strong> A 65 kg (143 lb) woman and a
    65 kg man calculate the same 3 g maintenance dose and the same 19.5 g loading dose. Four groups
    have body weight considerations worth stating directly.
  </p>

  <h3 class="mt-6 mb-2 text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)]">Creatine dosage for men</h3>
  <p class="mb-4 text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
    Men calculate 0.03 g/kg/day for maintenance. Higher average lean body mass places most men in the
    4–5 g range rather than the 3 g floor, and a 90 kg (198 lb) man running a loading phase takes
    25 g/day for one week.
  </p>

  <h3 class="mt-6 mb-2 text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)]">Creatine dosage for women</h3>
  <p class="mb-4 text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
    Women calculate the same 0.03 g/kg/day formula, which lands most women at 3 g/day. Women carry
    roughly 70–80% of the baseline muscle creatine stores that men carry, so the relative gain from
    supplementation is often larger. No separate women's formula exists — products marketed that way
    contain the same monohydrate. Our guide to
    <a href={url('/blog/creatine-for-women')} class={link}>creatine for women</a> covers the research on
    strength, body composition, and menstrual cycle timing.
  </p>

  <h3 class="mt-6 mb-2 text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)]">Creatine for teenagers</h3>
  <p class="mb-4 text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
    Teenagers under 18 need clearance from a physician or registered dietitian before starting
    creatine. The ISSN reports no adverse effects in supervised adolescent athletes at standard
    doses, and this site does not calculate doses for anyone under 18 without that supervision.
  </p>

  <h3 class="mt-6 mb-2 text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)]">
    Creatine dosage for vegetarians and vegans
  </h3>
  <p class="mb-4 text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
    Vegetarians and vegans start from a lower vegetarian baseline supplementation point, since
    dietary creatine comes from red meat and fish. Baseline muscle creatine runs 20–30% below that of
    omnivores, which produces a larger measured response to the same 3–5 g/day. The gram figure does
    not change.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mb-12 max-w-3xl rounded-2xl border border-[var(--tint-caution-border)] bg-[var(--tint-caution-bg)] p-6">
  <h3 class="mb-3 text-lg font-bold text-[var(--tint-caution-ink)]">Safety and side effects</h3>
  <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-[var(--tint-caution-ink)]">
    <li>
      <strong>Creatine does not harm the kidneys in healthy adults.</strong> Studies running up to 5 years
      at 3–5 g/day report no change in renal function. Anyone with existing kidney disease needs medical
      clearance first.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Creatine is not a steroid.</strong> Creatine is an amino acid derivative the liver and kidneys
      already produce at roughly 1–2 g per day.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Loading phase side effects are stomach-related.</strong> Bloating, cramping and 1–2 kg
      (2–4 lb) of water weight appear during loading and resolve on the maintenance dose.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Cycling creatine is not required.</strong> No published evidence shows receptor
      downregulation or a need for time off.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Take creatine on rest days.</strong> Saturation depends on daily intake, not training days.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-[var(--tint-caution-ink)]">
    This calculator provides educational information and does not constitute medical advice. Consult
    a qualified healthcare professional before starting any supplement, particularly with
    pre-existing conditions, kidney disease, pregnancy or breastfeeding.
  </p>
</section>
